# The calculus rules of the net, as the core C states them

Read 2026-11-20 from the core's header core.h (whole, 471 lines) and from net.c lines 1138–1498 and
1862–2609, at commit 3aed33d6. Every rule below was read at the lines given. Part of the workflow node
`core-forced-by-paper`. Nothing here is taken from any earlier description of the code.

## What a rule is

A term is a net of cells. Every cell has one principal port. A value provides at its principal port; an
operation consumes one value there. An active pair is an operation's principal port wired to a value's
principal port; its rule replaces the two cells by a net on their other wires (core.h 1–12). Two values,
or two operations, wired together are a wire nothing reads. A rule writes only the pair's two cells,
cells it makes, wires (by exchange) and slots of held values (by compare-and-swap) (core.h 291–297).
There are 45 kinds of value and 118 kinds of operation (core.h 97–281).

## The rules (operation meets value → result)

Line numbers are net.c.

Sharing and erasing
- duplicate meets a value with parts, or one not yet built → the value is held once in a box; each side
  gets a reference, read one level at a time (1147–1155)
- duplicate meets a value with no parts → two copies (1156–1162)
- erase meets a value → its parts are erased (1164–1169)
- any operation meets a reference → one level of the held value is copied out (1937–1942)
- any operation meets an unbuilt subterm → it is built (1933)
- a waiting read meets its value when it arrives → the reader gets its one-level copy; not a step (1865–1875)
- any operation meets a definition → the definition's value is copied in (1924–1931)

Functions, pairs, paths
- application meets a one-parameter λ → the body built with the argument (1962–1966)
- force meets a parameterless λ → the body (1990–1993)
- first, second meets a pair → that part; the other erased (1998–2001)
- path application meets a path λ → the body under the substitution of its dimension (2023–2028)
- path application at 0 or 1 with the endpoints at hand → that endpoint, without the path (2008–2012)
- path application at 0 or 1 meets a neutral path → the endpoint read from the path's type (2032–2041)
- any of these meets anything else that is not a system → a neutral holding the operands (1978–1986,
  1487–1497, 2043–2054)

Dimension substitution
- meets an interval or a face → substituted in normal form (1196–1197)
- meets a reference or an unbuilt subterm → carried by it, composed with what it already carries (1181–1192)
- meets a path λ → pushed under the binder, the binder renamed if the substitution's range mentions it (1198–1209)
- meets a system → the branch whose clause the substitution makes true; else the clauses substituted and
  split (1210–1244)
- meets a neutral → the neutral's own operation again, on the substituted parts, so it retries (1245–1259,
  1297–1318)
- meets a constructor of the calculus (λ, pair, Π, Σ, path type) → the same constructor, parts pending (1321–1327)
- meets a value with parts, not yet read → held in a box, the substitution pending on the reference (1342–1381)

Systems
- an eliminator meets a system → the operation branch by branch, the other operands restricted to each
  branch's clause, each deferred until that branch is read (1427–1483)
- adding a branch whose face is true → the branch; whose face is false → dropped (2264–2267)

Composition
- composition meets a tube that covers everything → the tube's end at 1 (2062–2067)
- composition with a partial tube → the type family at its own dimension; its head decides (2058–2061, 2071–2075)
- head Π → `%comp-pi`; Σ → `%comp-sigma`; path type → `%comp-path`; universe → `%comp-u`; Glue →
  `%comp-glue`; a composition of types → `%comp-gluel`: clauses written in the language (2091–2097,
  2117–2120, 2157)
- head a data type → that type's generated clause (2106–2116)
- head Word, empty tube → the base (2098–2105, 2162)
- head a system of families → each branch that holds along the whole line composes in its own family (2121–2148)
- head a neutral → a neutral composition (2149–2155)

Glue
- a Glue type whose system is total → the type on its face (2176)
- a glued value whose system is total → that value (2181)
- unglue on the face → the equivalence's map applied (2193–2196)
- unglue meets a glued value → its base (2200–2202)

Data types
- a path constructor with a dimension at 0 or 1, or whose boundary's face holds → its boundary
  (2321–2322, 2340–2343)
- composition in a data type, total tube → its end; partial tube → a formal composition, a value of the
  type (2353–2372)
- the same, type without path constructors, cap and every side the same constructor → into the fields
  (2373–2419)
- transport where the family is constant → the identity (2423–2426)
- transport meets a constructor → that constructor's generated clause (2465–2474)
- transport meets a formal composition → formal again, or the type's generated clause (2475–2491)
- case meets a constructor → the selected branch built with the fields, dimensions and shared variables,
  one step; each recursive field's hypothesis is the same case on that field, suspended (2509–2562)
- case meets a formal composition → a composition in the motive, by a clause written in the language (2563–2572)
- case meets anything else that is not a system → a neutral typed by the motive (2574–2588)

